Today I practised 5 LeetCode problems focusing on BST traversal, searching, insertion, pruning, and construction.

1. LeetCode 173 – Binary Search Tree Iterator

Difficulty: Medium

Concepts Covered:

Inorder Traversal

Stack

Iterator Design

BST Properties

Approach:

Use a stack to maintain the path to the next smallest node. Initially, push all left nodes. When next() is called, pop a node and push the left path of its right subtree.

class BSTIterator {
    Stack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<>();

    public BSTIterator(TreeNode root) {
        pushLeft(root);
    }

    private void pushLeft(TreeNode node) {
        while (node != null) {
            stack.push(node);
            node = node.left;
        }
    }

    public int next() {
        TreeNode node = stack.pop();
        pushLeft(node.right);
        return node.val;
    }

    public boolean hasNext() {
        return !stack.isEmpty();
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(1) amortized per operation.

Space Complexity: O(h), where h is the height of the tree.

2. LeetCode 669 – Trim a Binary Search Tree

Difficulty: Medium

Concepts Covered:

BST Properties

Recursion

Tree Pruning

DFS

Approach:

Remove nodes outside the range [low, high].

If the node value is smaller than low, continue with its right subtree.

If the node value is greater than high, continue with its left subtree.

Otherwise, recursively trim both subtrees.

class Solution {
    public TreeNode trimBST(TreeNode root, int low, int high) {
        if (root == null) return null;

        if (root.val < low) {
            return trimBST(root.right, low, high);
        }

        if (root.val > high) {
            return trimBST(root.left, low, high);
        }

        root.left = trimBST(root.left, low, high);
        root.right = trimBST(root.right, low, high);

        return root;
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(n) worst case.

Space Complexity: O(h), recursive stack.

3. LeetCode 700 – Search in a Binary Search Tree

Difficulty: Easy

Concepts Covered:

BST Properties

Recursion

Searching

Approach:

Compare the target value with the current node. Move left if the target is smaller, move right if it is greater, and return the node if the value matches.

class Solution {
    public TreeNode searchBST(TreeNode root, int val) {
        if (root == null || root.val == val) {
            return root;
        }

        if (val < root.val) {
            return searchBST(root.left, val);
        }

        return searchBST(root.right, val);
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(h).

Space Complexity: O(h), recursive stack.

4. LeetCode 701 – Insert into a Binary Search Tree

Difficulty: Medium

Concepts Covered:

BST Properties

Recursion

Insertion

Tree Manipulation

Approach:

Find the correct position for the new value by comparing it with the current node. Insert the new node when a null position is reached.

class Solution {
    public TreeNode insertIntoBST(TreeNode root, int val) {
        if (root == null) {
            return new TreeNode(val);
        }

        if (val < root.val) {
            root.left = insertIntoBST(root.left, val);
        } else {
            root.right = insertIntoBST(root.right, val);
        }

        return root;
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(h).

Space Complexity: O(h), recursive stack.

5. LeetCode 108 – Convert Sorted Array to Binary Search Tree

Difficulty: Easy

Concepts Covered:

Divide and Conquer

Recursion

BST Construction

Balanced Binary Tree

Approach:

Choose the middle element of the sorted array as the root. Recursively construct the left and right subtrees using the left and right portions of the array.

class Solution {
    public TreeNode sortedArrayToBST(int[] nums) {
        return buildTree(nums, 0, nums.length - 1);
    }

    private TreeNode buildTree(int[] nums, int left, int right) {
        if (left > right) return null;

        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(nums[mid]);

        root.left = buildTree(nums, left, mid - 1);
        root.right = buildTree(nums, mid + 1, right);

        return root;
    }
}

Time Complexity: O(n).

Space Complexity: O(log n) recursive stack for the balanced tree.

